ght covet in france have  ended that includes everything from  curfews emergency lockdowns remote  working the wearing of face masks and  holding a health pass to access museums  or restaurants  the lifting of covet restrictions has  been welcomed by many  especially at paris's tourist hot spots  after a two-year break visitors are once  again flocking to the french capital  i don't think imposing restrictions on  people is a good thing we're all human  and we're all free it goes against  personal liberty  on the streets it's so much nicer now to  see people's faces to see them smiling  is so much more livable but others  remain wary  we're still afraid of the new variant of  the coronovirus  a few days ago we've been to disneyland  which is so crowded and nobody  was worried nobody was wearing a mask  health authorities are still monitoring  new infections and the end of the state  of health emergency has not changed  france's policy on testing  this pharmacy says the number of people  coming in for covet tests has dropped  drastically  but that could change once parisians  return from the summer holidays  the restrictions should be lifted  progressively step by step and not end  it overnight there are places like  public transportation or pharmacies  where the mask is recommended but not  mandatory but it should be we have to  take care of people who are vulnerable  and at times seriously sick i strongly  expect a rise in positive covet cases  for his part isn't taking any chances he  preps his taxi carefully before taking  on his next passenger damia has not  contracted kovitz so far  and he intends to keep it that way  finally freedom serenity hope for a  return to normal life or should we  remain cautious about the risk of  infection dw reporter stephanie torbel  talked to epidemiologist ralph ranches  from the university of applied sciences  in hamburg he's part of a european task  force that monitors pandemics  [Music]  in france many of the current  coronavirus restrictions were lifted on  august 1st what do you think of that  that was a political decision  on the one hand one can really  understand the people who are sick and  tired of the pandemic  but of course at the end of the summer  wave it will make it easier for the  virus to spread in france  with all the restrictions lifted how can  people be persuaded to wear masks again  in the fall if infection numbers rise  what's really really important is a  clear risk communication strategy  so that in the fall it's made absolutely  clear how high the risk of reinfection  is in order to motivate people to  protect themselves properly  what would a risk communication strategy  look like  and clearly showing how the virus is  spread  and by urging the need for cooperation  collaboration from all parties to  control its spread  meaning that we're in the same boat and  that we want to move forward and not  experience another extremely big wave in  the fall and winter  in germany ministers have proposed a  road map for the fall from october 1st  it will be mandatory to wear an ffp2  mask for long distance travel and on  airplanes in germany as well as a mask  and test mandate in hospitals and care  facilities do you think that makes sense  it certainly makes a lot of sense right  now a lot of people are coming close to  one another on public transport on a  regular basis often over extended  periods of time  now on trains and airplanes people sit  next to each other for long periods of  time and that's where a mask mandate is  extremely helpful  it would be great if there would be a  nationwide mask mandate on local public  transport too and in places where  vulnerable people live naturally it  makes sense not to introduce viruses  there  does it make sense that politicians have  already ruled out further closures and  lockdowns  that's a very very good question we're  all hoping that these measures won't  become necessary again  but we can't be 100 sure how the  situation will look in fall and winter  and then of course from an  epidemiological perspective we can't  rule out any measures at the moment  rather try to do everything possible to  avoid it  what would you do differently if you  were making the decisions i've been  well i'm a scientist and i'm trying to  make sense of the pandemic and proceed  accordingly  i would very much like to try that with  a different risk communication strategy  to further increase trust  so that we can all enjoy our freedom  with the right awareness and the  knowledge and also know where our limits  are where the dangers lie  and i think mask mandates indoors for  example in all public buildings in the  fall and winter would certainly make a  lot of sense  sciences in hamburg  you're welcome  paraguay became a target destination for  german vaccination opponents during the  pandemic  some even moved there to avoid getting  vaccinated  in their supposed paradise however these  germans are not particularly welcome  [Music]  paraguay's relaxed coveted rules have  seen german anti-vaxxers flock to the  country  there have even been cases where a  parent has taken their child to paraguay  without the other parent knowing all to  escape the vaccine  overall several thousand germans have  arrived in the south american country  which has one of the world's worst  health systems  stefan schweiz is a german paraguayan  lawyer he helped one mother who spent  six months looking for her daughter  after she was abducted by her father  this is no isolated case it was a  particularly sensitive one but we have  several in paraguay  he says many come without knowing  anything about paraguay and then  struggle  there are plenty of quite extreme cases  where people arrive in paraguay without  knowing anything of the language or the  conditions here  in my opinion it's just crazy  they leave everything of their old life  behind  they sell their houses  load up a container and just turn up  here  and many of them come with children too  and in paraguay it's not that easy to  find a job for example even if you do  speak the language  well over 1600 new arrivals from germany  were processed by paraguay's immigration  authorities in 2021 that's nearly three  times as many as the year before  the numbers have shot up over the last  two years because of the coronavirus  but political events have also played a  role  like the war in ukraine  which many of these people are worried  and yet about  is not safer than germany we spoke to  guillermo zakira an epidemiologist in  charge of monitoring national health  if they're hoping for good health care  and a well-functioning  our health system is one of the most  precarious in the americas  i can see that people coming from  countries like germany and austria are  going to suffer here  paraguay is experiencing a fourth wave  of covet 19 but with death rates lower  than in previous waves saket is  concerned about the german influx for  another reason  epidemics that have not existed in  paraguay for more than 20 years could be  reintroduced  things like measles for example  or polio which we haven't had for more  than 30 years  these are diseases that people who are  not vaccinated can reintroduce when you  don't have a good health system  we've come to the german paraguayan  cultural institute its director zimona  hendrich moved here from germany 22  years ago she too is concerned about the  current trend  i follow some channels on telegram  i find some of the things posted by the  germans who come here quite scary  she's vaccinated against covert 19 to  protect herself and others she says many  people arriving from germany come to her  asking for a job but they don't want to  get vaccinated  it's a very diverse group too very large  with different religions different  opinions  but it has a dark side to it too  the new arrivals mean the german  community here is much more diverse lars  classen grew up here he's a computer  programmer of german mennonite origin  i know people here in paraguay who  oppose the vaccine  and they have their reasons  but they're in the minority  most of my friends are in favor of the  vaccine  he himself has three coveted vaccine  shots just like his wife and children  i wouldn't describe paraguay as any kind  of paradise for germans who oppose  science  and of course not all germans coming to  paraguay do  the contact restrictions during the  covert pandemic had massive social  consequences for many people  in poorer neighborhoods of the  argentinian capital buenos aires  neighbours set up community support  groups and the idea became a huge  success both in the fight against kovid  and for social cohesion  in 2020 in the midst of the coronavirus  pandemic residents and experts created a  crisis committee in the mujica villa 31  suburb of buenos aires it was set up to  address the needs of the locals the  committee was even recognised by the  government  founding member silvana oliveira tells  us about some of the group's biggest  successes including el tetectar  el detector was the name of a coveted  testing truck that the government sent  around the various neighborhoods  he could walk up get swapped and you'd  have your result within 24 hours  if it was positive you'd be taken to a  coveted hotel to isolate  and we had free  canteens those are one of the group's  biggest achievements  thanks to the committee these free  neighborhood canteens multiplied and  strengthened during the pandemic  they were funded by government subsidies  and donations from private institutions  we weren't given much food  only enough for 57 portions  but we always made more than 57 portions  because so many people had lost their  jobs during the pandemic  we had hundreds of people looking for  food  for decades the city government has been  trying to strategically expel people  from the mohica neighborhood  it's located very close to the financial  district and the port the most lucrative  areas of buenos aires which makes it all  the more important for people here to  unite and mobilize  if there's one thing that people in poor  neighborhoods of argentina are known for  or in any vulnerable community in the  world in fact it's self-organization  the committee couldn't meet during the  first wave of covert due to the  restrictions imposed by the argentinian  government  but the cristo obrero church then  offered its facilities to the group to  meet under strict covert requirements  it was here that most of the suggestions  put forward by the committee were set in  motion  priest guillermo torres was in charge at  the time  it was like a network we had shelters  some people cooked some gave health  advice others looked after the elderly  at home the committee also brought a lot  of issues to light that people didn't  know about only the residents know the  true reality  the muhika via 31 neighborhood is home  to most of the capital's manual workers  and day labourers  forming organised groups is especially  important in these impoverished areas of  the country because they make the  problems that burden them more visible  today our committee is strong  we clearly set an example of how to  fight for your neighborhood  because other crisis committees were  later set up elsewhere  this was the start of the fight for  health rights in poor neighborhoods  during the pandemic  the neighborhood is currently undergoing  a redevelopment project which is  followed and monitored by the committee  the group hopes to continue their work  after the pandemic  what would you like to know about kovid  our science editor derek williams has  the lowdown on the latest research and  analysis  just write to covertproducer dw.com  [Music]  this week he answers the question  do some people have genes that give them  advantages against covid19  this is a super interesting question and  and one where lots of research is going  on in a lot of different areas um let's  look at a few of them starting with  blood types which of course are  genetically determined  since the early days of the pandemic  there have been  many many studies on whether your blood  group could play a role in covid  severity  or mortality like this fairly recent one  from researchers at king's college in  the uk  which looked at three thousand different  proteins found in blood now it found  that six variants seem to make the  disease worse if you have them while  eight variants  seem to make it better but there's of  course no smoking gun that says one  blood type  saves or condemns you people of all  blood types can and do get the disease  another area where genes could play a  role is in how they impact on immune  response  a recent study by german researchers  that looked at that question  found that a particular variant of a  gene called  gnb3 that it seems to be linked to lower  mortality now gnb3  plays a role in activating immune cells  so it would kind of make sense if a  highly effective variant of it provided  some clear protective benefits um other  genes linked to the immune system also  almost  certainly feed into risk for example  those that make some people more likely  to develop diabetes or those that make  people more likely to experience a  life-threatening cytokine storm if they  catch the disease  finally there's the idea that some  people might actually be genetically  immune to covet 19.  hypothetical territory we know for  example that having highly specific  variants of a particular gene that that  makes a small group of people basically  immune  to hiv  they simply don't catch it  might there be  similar genetic variants that could  somehow prevent someone from  catching covid  maybe  one area that's being looked at closely  is what's called the ace2 receptor  that's a protein  on a cell's surface  that the virus subverts to break in now  if someone's genes cause them to produce  an altered version of ace2 it could at  least in theory  prevent the virus from latching on  making that person effectively immune  now if that kind of genetic variant  exists a big if  then the group of people who have it is  likely very small but it would be great  to discover something like that because  it would give researchers designing  medications against the disease a really  massive  foot forward just as it did with  designing medications  for treating hiv  [Music]  when colombia's capital bogota was in  lockdown venezuelan migrants in  particular had little chance of finding  a job  workshops run by a local charity aimed  to solve that  in a sewing workshop some very special  clothes were created with each garment  also representing a chance for a better  life  [Music]  one of the most dangerous neighborhoods  in bogota whimsical dresses are on  display  they were made during the pandemic and  tell of the dreams of venezuelan migrant  women  one in particular stands out in the  colors of the flags of colombia and  venezuela  at the sewing box we made the dress of  dreams it includes people from venezuela  as well as colombia  nationality doesn't matter  it means that we're all united by the  dress  we asked the women at the sewing box to  write messages that spoke to everyone's  dreams  and then we all started writing down our  dreams and sewing them together  the dress is an example of a sewing box  collaboration created after the lockdown  during the coveted waves the 15 women  who worked on the dress had been badly  affected by the covet restrictions  the sewing box is an initiative of the  procreate foundation  during lockdown many migrant women  experienced violence in their shelters  we were looking for a sanctuary  the sewing box gives women the  opportunity to have a safe place and an  income and to put their sewing ideas  into action such as designing symbolic  dresses  they can start a small business and earn  money for what they do  during lockdown the sewing box was  forced to close  when it reopened the women here set to  work making masks and protective suits  when they told us we could return to the  sewing box i was so happy  i said  let's release the doves  we'll fly again and here we are now  meeting and talking with one another  for many women here the sewing box is a  refuge from the problems in bogota's  neighborhoods  [Music]  the neighborhood here isn't as dear to  me as my original home in venezuela but  the sewing box is when i sew i get  distance from the problems in the area  then it's as if i'm not here as if this  neighborhood didn't exist  [Music]  with the money they earned after the  lockdown the women have begun to realize  their dream to be able to make a living  from sewing and designing clothes  i started to work  i bought a sewing machine with the money  i earned  i call it senor paper  it's paper but it sometimes gets jammed  now the women are sewing uniforms and  applying what they learned during the  pandemic  sandra ulya was their teacher  fashion is always new  it never ends  there are always new materials new  designs  everyone wants something new  that's why it never ends  new ideas for clothes were born in the  pandemics  lots of businesses went bust during the  pandemic but the sewing box made it  through  always keep going for us  the women will continue to meet here at  their refuge it makes them feel like  they're part of something very special  and that's all from this week's covert  19 special thanks for watching and see  you next time  [Music]  you

